+      "id": "T3113",
+      "title": "Investigate FUSE filesystem claim - verify atomic_write() works on all target filesystems",
+      "status": "done",
+      "priority": "medium",
+      "type": "task",
+      "parentId": "T1342",
+      "createdAt": "2026-02-06T16:04:05Z",
+      "updatedAt": "2026-02-06T18:28:05Z",
+      "size": "medium",
+      "position": 30,
+      "positionVersion": 0,
+      "phase": "core",
+      "description": "Config investigator claimed mv is not atomic on FUSE. Need to verify: (1) Is this actually true for fuseblk/fuse2fs? (2) Does our atomic_write() in lib/file-ops.sh handle this correctly? (3) What filesystems do we support? (4) Do we need read-after-write verification? Must not have works on my machine bugs.",
+      "labels": [
+        "data-integrity",
+        "portability",
+        "research"
+      ],
+      "completedAt": "2026-02-06T18:28:05Z",
+      "notes": [
+        "[COMPLETED 2026-02-06T18:28:05Z] RESEARCH COMPLETE: FUSE atomicity claim is a red herring for CLEO's use case.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n### 1. Is rename() atomic on FUSE?\n\n**Same-directory rename IS atomic on all major FUSE implementations** for the in-memory/VFS layer. POSIX specifies that rename() must atomically replace the target if it exists. This guarantee is honored by:\n- **ext4, APFS, HFS+, btrfs**: Fully atomic same-directory rename\n- **FUSE (fuseblk, fuse2fs, ntfs-3g)**: Atomic from userspace perspective (the FUSE kernel module serializes operations)\n- **SSHFS**: Known exception - SFTP protocol does NOT support atomic rename-over-existing. Workaround: -o workaround=rename (makes it non-atomic but functional)\n- **NFS**: Historically problematic, modern implementations are better\n\n**Cross-directory rename is NOT guaranteed atomic** on any filesystem. However, CLEO does NOT do cross-directory renames.\n\n### 2. What does CLEO's atomic_write() actually do?\n\nThe implementation in lib/atomic-write.sh (aw_atomic_write) does:\n1. aw_write_temp() - writes to file.tmp IN THE SAME DIRECTORY as target\n2. aw_create_backup() - copies original to backups/operational/\n3. aw_atomic_move() - mv (rename) from file.tmp to file IN THE SAME DIRECTORY\n\n**This is correct.** The temp file is always created alongside the target (line 150: local temp_file=target_file._AW_TEMP_SUFFIX), ensuring same-filesystem, same-directory rename. This is the gold standard pattern for atomic writes.\n\n### 3. Filesystems CLEO must support\n\n- ext4 (Linux): SAFE - fully atomic same-dir rename\n- APFS/HFS+ (macOS): SAFE - fully atomic same-dir rename\n- FUSE mounts (WSL uses drvfs/9p, not FUSE): SAFE for same-dir rename\n- NTFS-3g via FUSE: SAFE - implements rename correctly\n- SSHFS: PARTIAL - rename-over-existing not atomic without workaround. But nobody should run a task manager on an SSHFS mount.\n- NFS: GENERALLY SAFE for same-dir rename on modern implementations\n\n### 4. Is read-after-write verification needed?\n\n**No.** Adding post-rename checks like [[ -f target ]] && [[ -s target ]] would NOT help:\n- If rename() returns success, the VFS layer guarantees the file is at the new path\n- A post-check could race with another writer (though CLEO uses flock)\n- The real durability concern is crash recovery (fsync), not rename atomicity\n- Performance cost would be minimal but provides zero value\n\n### 5. Was the config.json deletion caused by FUSE?\n\n**No - the FUSE theory was a red herring.** The test bug found in tests/unit/lifecycle-enforcement.bats line 241 does rm -f on the real config.json. This is the actual root cause. The test was deleting config.json as part of its test scenario but operating on the real project directory instead of a test fixture.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**No changes needed to atomic_write().** The implementation is correct:\n- Same-directory temp file creation (prevents cross-filesystem issues)\n- flock-based locking (prevents concurrent write races)\n- Backup before move (enables rollback)\n- Proper error handling with cleanup\n\nThe only scenario where CLEO's atomic_write could fail is SSHFS without the rename workaround, which is not a supported use case for a local task manager.\n\nVERDICT: The FUSE concern was a red herring. The config.json deletion was caused by the test bug (rm -f on real config.json), not by filesystem atomicity issues."
